WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:55.670
- Hi everyone, Sal Khan here

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:56.503
from Khan Academy.

00:00:56.503 --> 00:00:59.450
Welcome to our daily live stream.

00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:00.860
This is a way we've started

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:02.150
this a couple of weeks,

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:03.730
actually a months ago now.

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:04.900
As way for all of us

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:07.239
to take connected during times

00:01:07.239 --> 00:01:08.380
of social distancing and school closures.

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:09.700
Then it's, we're just having

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:10.910
an interesting conversation.

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:12.460
We have a lot of interesting guests

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:14.190
and today we will just be doing

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:15.950
an ask me anything,

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:17.640
where you can literally ask me anything

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.820
for those of y'all on YouTube or Facebook.

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:21.020
Start putting your questions in.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:21.853
I have team members who

00:01:21.853 --> 00:01:24.940
are helping me out surfacing
questions to answer,

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:27.840
and I'm, literally happy
to answer anything.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:29.520
Before we start,

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:30.460
I want to give a special,

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:31.960
thanks to several corporate partners

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:34.110
who have allowed us to
do what we're doing,

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:37.460
especially with our response to COVID-19,

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:38.530
special thanks to Bank of America,

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:39.880
Google.org, AT and T,

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:41.180
Fastly, and Novartis.

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:42.070
We were running a deficit

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:43.300
even before this crisis.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:44.133
And you can imagine

00:01:44.133 --> 00:01:45.960
our server costs have
gone up dramatically.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:46.830
We're trying to accelerate

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:48.650
a whole series of programs.

00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:49.810
So this support has helped a lot,

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:50.800
and special thanks to the many

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:52.410
other foundations and corporations

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:55.920
that have helped us well
beyond before COVID.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:57.210
But I do like to emphasize that

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:58.600
we are still running in a deficit.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:00.221
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:02:00.221 --> 00:02:01.640
please think about making

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:02.860
a donation to Khan Academy.

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:05.620
Every donation makes a difference.

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:07.200
So with that let's,

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:10.661
just jump into asking me anything,

00:02:10.661 --> 00:02:12.230
for whatever that's worth.

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:13.063
It looks like there

00:02:13.063 --> 00:02:15.300
are some questions that have come in.

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:18.970
So, Jason Dunlap from Facebook asks,

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:21.590
Hey, Mr. Khan member of
Khan Academy since 2011.

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:23.370
So Jason, you've been with us

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:24.880
since near the beginning.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:26.410
Is there a particular subject matter

00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:28.050
with which you struggled?

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:29.763
How did you deal with it?

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:33.820
I think the first time

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:36.170
that I really found myself struggling

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:37.083
with a class.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:40.470
I was a student

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:43.380
that tended to do really,

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:46.930
I was academically doing
fine most of my career,

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:48.331
but then once I got into high school,

00:02:48.331 --> 00:02:50.560
I got really,

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:51.780
I wanted to accelerate even more.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:52.613
I got very,

00:02:52.613 --> 00:02:54.620
I guess you could say
mathematically ambitious.

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:57.120
And I think I was my sophomore year.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:59.100
I went to the local university

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:01.000
and I started taking classes there.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:04.800
And the first few classes
I found quite straight,

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:06.020
they didn't cause too much trouble.

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:06.853
But I remember when I took

00:03:06.853 --> 00:03:08.870
that first real analysis course,

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:09.703
For those of y'all,

00:03:09.703 --> 00:03:11.570
who aren't familiar with real analysis,

00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:13.230
that's kind of the first real,

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:16.130
like mathematically math course

00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:17.880
where we go really deep into proofs.

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:21.190
You go really deep into the analysis of,

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:22.260
the real number system

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:23.930
and functions and all of that.

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:25.590
And I think that switching gears

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:27.280
from what you typically do

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:29.240
in most of your math,

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:31.810
your high school career of

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:33.550
where you're learning about,

00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:35.290
for sure a lot of concepts and procedures,

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:38.073
but you're not as mathematically rigorous.

00:03:39.494 --> 00:03:42.160
I guess you could say
real mathematicians are.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:43.120
That actually did cause

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:44.190
a little bit of like,

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:45.023
I remember there was

00:03:45.023 --> 00:03:46.068
a couple of weeks

00:03:46.068 --> 00:03:46.910
where I just like,

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:48.840
of course a leads A to B,

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:49.906
why do I have to prove it?

00:03:49.906 --> 00:03:51.600
But then once I got over it,

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.920
I definitely started really enjoying it.

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:54.880
And that's part of the reason

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:56.420
why I eventually became a math major,

00:03:56.420 --> 00:03:57.253
because I saw the beauty

00:03:57.253 --> 00:04:00.780
in that kind of real, formal logic.

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:02.740
But that was definitely

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:04.890
a time that I at least,

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:07.570
for a period struggled a bit

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:10.730
there's, let's see if there's other causes

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:16.940
that I really had difficult.

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:20.610
As I went, in my Khan Academy mode,

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:22.800
part of the fun of my job,

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:25.630
is being able to refresh

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:27.640
or learn new subjects

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:29.160
to be able to teach it better.

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:31.890
And, organic chemistry is interesting

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:33.910
because I did take it in college.

00:04:33.910 --> 00:04:34.972
And when I took it in college,

00:04:34.972 --> 00:04:35.990
I learned it.

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:37.650
I did fine in the course,

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:40.130
but when we were going to
cover it on Khan Academy,

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:41.010
I really wanted

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:41.880
to be able to do it

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.180
in a way that was as intuitive

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:45.480
as what we could do in math

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:47.160
or physics or regular chemistry

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:48.570
or any other subject.

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:50.760
And it did take me a little bit of time

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:53.270
to get that intuition
for organic chemistry,

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:56.000
because the way it's typically taught

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:58.130
the way it's typically
surfaced in textbooks,

00:04:58.130 --> 00:04:58.963
it just feels like

00:04:58.963 --> 00:05:00.604
a bunch of random mechanisms that you,

00:05:00.604 --> 00:05:03.670
that a lot of students
just go through a memorize.

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:06.090
But when you really
start to think about it,

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:09.180
there are some very beautiful themes

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:10.630
or concepts that transcend all

00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:11.930
of organic chemistry.

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:13.590
It's all about electronegativity,

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:15.500
which atom and a molecule

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:17.120
is the one that really wants

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:19.060
to hug those electrons,

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:21.720
really wants those electrons closer to it.

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:22.880
And from then,

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:24.300
you actually can deduce,

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:25.800
a lot of the mechanisms.

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:27.450
You don't have to memorize them all,

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:29.670
or even if you do memorize some of them,

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:31.690
you can kind of see the intuition

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:34.556
of why the universe does what it does,

00:05:34.556 --> 00:05:37.290
because of the electronegativity.

00:05:37.290 --> 00:05:39.070
But that was definitely a little bit of,

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:40.378
it took me a little bit of time

00:05:40.378 --> 00:05:43.103
to start to see that thread line.

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:45.650
There's other things,

00:05:45.650 --> 00:05:47.970
there's definitely parts of biology

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:48.803
where I'm like,

00:05:48.803 --> 00:05:49.880
well, why is that lead to that?

00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:51.063
And I would start calling up friends

00:05:51.063 --> 00:05:53.790
and people who are
professors in the subject.

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:54.623
And then they would say,

00:05:54.623 --> 00:05:56.140
well actually that's an
open area of research.

00:05:56.140 --> 00:05:56.973
And so I oftentimes said,

00:05:56.973 --> 00:05:58.270
why don't they just tell everyone

00:05:58.270 --> 00:05:59.324
at the textbook that we don't know

00:05:59.324 --> 00:06:01.440
that that's an area of research,

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:02.390
because I actually think that

00:06:02.390 --> 00:06:04.200
is more satisfying to know that,

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:06.270
okay, no one actually has the intuition

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:08.880
for exactly why that leads to that.

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:09.713
And by the way,

00:06:09.713 --> 00:06:10.546
if you're asking that question,

00:06:10.546 --> 00:06:12.200
you're already at the
frontiers of research,

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:14.569
which I think is a pretty cool idea,

00:06:14.569 --> 00:06:16.530
but yeah, let's see.

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:18.490
Facebook, Miguel Hernandez asked,

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:20.270
what is your biggest challenge now

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:22.899
as the leader of Khan Academy?

00:06:22.899 --> 00:06:24.680
Great question, Miguel.

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:27.900
I think there's a lot of layers to that.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:31.560
I think, I would say

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:33.700
the overarching question

00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:35.381
that sometimes keeps me up at night is,

00:06:35.381 --> 00:06:40.190
if Khan Academy from 10 years ago,

00:06:40.190 --> 00:06:41.330
so Khan Academy to do,

00:06:41.330 --> 00:06:43.580
so Khan Academy served from 10 years ago,

00:06:43.580 --> 00:06:45.350
saw Khan Academy today.

00:06:45.350 --> 00:06:46.183
He would say,

00:06:46.183 --> 00:06:47.284
wow, that thing that started in

00:06:47.284 --> 00:06:49.230
this walk-in closet

00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:51.150
got much bigger than expected

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:51.983
to see that

00:06:51.983 --> 00:06:55.630
we're over 200 folks
employees around the world.

00:06:55.630 --> 00:06:57.000
We have translation projects

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:58.440
where across subject and grade

00:06:58.440 --> 00:06:59.900
that served from 10 years ago

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:00.890
would have been overjoyed.

00:07:00.890 --> 00:07:01.723
We're like, wow,

00:07:01.723 --> 00:07:03.520
Khan Academy has kind of

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:04.710
had escape velocity.

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:05.870
It's able to have the impact

00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:07.550
that it was just a delusion,

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:09.600
just a dream 10 years ago.

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:11.240
But I think now,

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:12.420
I feel like,

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:13.530
I guess you could say a stress

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:17.570
or an urgency that we don't squander

00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:18.930
the opportunity that we have.

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:20.154
It's great that Khan Academy

00:07:20.154 --> 00:07:22.390
has a hundred million registered users

00:07:22.390 --> 00:07:24.310
and it serves tens of
millions every month,

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:26.220
but there's billions of people

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:29.090
who need to be empowered and,

00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:30.610
not just on a superficial level,

00:07:30.610 --> 00:07:32.800
but we want to be able
to empower them deeply

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:34.500
across subjects and grades

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:35.660
and create ways for them

00:07:35.660 --> 00:07:37.450
to plug into the broader system.

00:07:37.450 --> 00:07:40.140
And there's just still
a lot of work to do.

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:42.330
And, even with this COVID crisis,

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:44.740
there's a lot that's
negative about this crisis.

00:07:44.740 --> 00:07:45.870
The silver lining is that

00:07:45.870 --> 00:07:46.845
there's an open mindedness

00:07:46.845 --> 00:07:49.470
to thinking about all things,

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:51.080
but especially education in new ways.

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:53.053
And so I sometimes,

00:07:53.053 --> 00:07:55.313
there's a tension between,

00:07:56.760 --> 00:07:58.620
this is a marathon at the end of the day.

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:01.600
Like, the world isn't
gonna change overnight,

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:03.540
but there's moments where there's openness

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:04.620
to new solutions

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:05.550
and I'm always thinking about,

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:07.210
well, how can we Khan Academy

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:09.230
really serve people right now,

00:08:09.230 --> 00:08:11.040
right when the need is urgent.

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:12.960
So I think that's kind of the high level.

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:14.300
The biggest tension

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:17.550
is how do you balance between moving fast

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:18.390
and being scrappy

00:08:18.390 --> 00:08:19.570
and ultra entrepreneurial

00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:21.793
with there's a lot of,

00:08:23.210 --> 00:08:24.570
Khan Academy has a lot of users now,

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:26.250
you have to bring them along.

00:08:26.250 --> 00:08:27.300
We have a large team,

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:28.840
we got to make sure that we're aligned,

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:32.000
that we're all kind of
marching in the same direction,

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:34.710
so that we can use the resources

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:36.534
that folks are donating
to us as well as possible.

00:08:36.534 --> 00:08:39.540
I think one double click below that

00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:40.530
it's making sure that

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:41.760
we have the resources

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:43.270
to do what we wanna do

00:08:43.270 --> 00:08:44.750
and that we can treat it

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:46.020
as a marathon.

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:47.460
That we do have visibility

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:48.640
over not just this year

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:49.690
or the next several years,

00:08:49.690 --> 00:08:50.710
but that we have the visibility

00:08:50.710 --> 00:08:51.970
to be able to plan

00:08:51.970 --> 00:08:53.070
and actually create

00:08:53.070 --> 00:08:54.130
what the world needs

00:08:54.130 --> 00:08:56.900
and not just follow the latest trend

00:08:56.900 --> 00:08:59.290
or whatever seems to
be hot at that moment.

00:08:59.290 --> 00:09:01.110
I think that's another thing

00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:01.943
that I think a lot about.

00:09:01.943 --> 00:09:04.030
And then you double click below that,

00:09:04.030 --> 00:09:04.970
and that gets a little bit more

00:09:04.970 --> 00:09:07.100
to the tactical level at Khan Academy.

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:07.933
And this is,

00:09:07.933 --> 00:09:09.910
every year and every front few months,

00:09:09.910 --> 00:09:13.280
we have spirited conversations
about prioritizing.

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:14.113
And that's hard

00:09:14.113 --> 00:09:14.946
because when you have

00:09:14.946 --> 00:09:15.838
a limited amount of resources

00:09:15.838 --> 00:09:17.820
and you thinking about

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:19.010
the scope of what Khan Academy

00:09:19.010 --> 00:09:20.530
is trying to do,

00:09:20.530 --> 00:09:22.330
there's things that,

00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:23.590
we're able to do

00:09:23.590 --> 00:09:24.580
and improve on every year.

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:25.413
And then there's things

00:09:25.413 --> 00:09:26.670
that are super valuable

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:28.100
that we just don't have the resources.

00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:28.933
We just don't have

00:09:28.933 --> 00:09:30.250
the capacity to do every year.

00:09:30.250 --> 00:09:31.794
And that's always a
little bit heartbreaking

00:09:31.794 --> 00:09:33.111
and that's always hard

00:09:33.111 --> 00:09:38.111
to kind of thread the
needle on that level.

00:09:38.490 --> 00:09:39.323
But when you really think

00:09:39.323 --> 00:09:40.760
about all of the dimensions of conduct,

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:42.520
I mean, I have to remind
myself sometimes too,

00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:43.710
because I'm sometimes when the mind says,

00:09:43.710 --> 00:09:44.850
Oh yeah, it was a thing.

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:45.970
We have the software.

00:09:45.970 --> 00:09:46.844
But if you think about,

00:09:46.844 --> 00:09:47.927
we're trying to cover,

00:09:47.927 --> 00:09:50.480
13 or 14 years grade levels,

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:52.780
we have Khan Academy
kids we're covering now,

00:09:52.780 --> 00:09:53.700
not just math,

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:56.205
but we're doing english and language arts,

00:09:56.205 --> 00:09:58.820
reading, social-emotional learning,

00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:01.630
the sciences, the humanities

00:10:01.630 --> 00:10:03.100
we're doing things like test prep

00:10:03.100 --> 00:10:05.000
for the SAT for the LSAT.

00:10:06.060 --> 00:10:07.620
And we want to explore ways

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:09.140
to work deeper with districts,

00:10:09.140 --> 00:10:10.990
give them things like district dashboards,

00:10:10.990 --> 00:10:13.500
run efficacy studies and,

00:10:13.500 --> 00:10:15.290
each of these things
you can double click on

00:10:15.290 --> 00:10:16.250
and have a bunch of things

00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:17.083
that you have to figure out

00:10:17.083 --> 00:10:19.650
to do right all while
tens of millions of folks

00:10:19.650 --> 00:10:20.883
are using the platform.

00:10:22.450 --> 00:10:24.070
It can get complex sometimes.

00:10:24.070 --> 00:10:25.950
So I think navigating that complexity,

00:10:25.950 --> 00:10:26.940
But with that said,

00:10:26.940 --> 00:10:28.380
no one should feel sorry for me.

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:30.720
I consider myself incredibly lucky

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:31.851
to work with this team,

00:10:31.851 --> 00:10:33.100
to work with all of

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:34.850
the community at Khan Academy,

00:10:34.850 --> 00:10:36.430
to work with the hundreds
of thousands of folks

00:10:36.430 --> 00:10:38.290
who donated the Khan Academy

00:10:38.290 --> 00:10:41.483
to be able to work on what I think is,

00:10:42.790 --> 00:10:44.244
kind of the central issue.

00:10:44.244 --> 00:10:46.490
Issue of education,

00:10:46.490 --> 00:10:47.820
even some of the issues that

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:49.940
we're dealing with in the world right now,

00:10:49.940 --> 00:10:51.420
especially in the US right now,

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:53.320
I talked at length about it on Monday.

00:10:54.873 --> 00:10:57.500
Education can empower those who

00:10:57.500 --> 00:11:00.210
are oppressed and it
can enlighten those who

00:11:00.210 --> 00:11:02.590
are oppressors, because I do think

00:11:02.590 --> 00:11:04.150
that most people in the world

00:11:05.090 --> 00:11:07.560
are good or can be good.

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:10.110
But they've, might've had weird thoughts

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:11.860
or programming or experiences

00:11:11.860 --> 00:11:15.150
that maybe make them deviate

00:11:15.150 --> 00:11:18.350
from what would otherwise
be their good selves.

00:11:18.350 --> 00:11:19.470
And I think education

00:11:19.470 --> 00:11:21.570
is the way to really unlock

00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:22.640
and enlightened people

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:24.400
and to see the humanity in others

00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:27.130
and to give everyone the critical thinking

00:11:27.130 --> 00:11:29.410
so that they can help navigate

00:11:29.410 --> 00:11:30.850
their own lives in a positive way,

00:11:30.850 --> 00:11:32.740
but also participate in a democracy.

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:35.620
And, democracy is just a magical thing,

00:11:35.620 --> 00:11:36.950
that's always gonna lead to good

00:11:36.950 --> 00:11:39.410
in order for democracy to work,

00:11:39.410 --> 00:11:40.710
the people voting

00:11:40.710 --> 00:11:42.400
and the people that they're voting in

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:44.160
need to have be educated,

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:46.750
have really strong
critical thinking skills,

00:11:46.750 --> 00:11:49.320
be able to see issues
from multiple dimensions,

00:11:49.320 --> 00:11:50.740
be able to empathize with folks

00:11:50.740 --> 00:11:52.310
who are not like themselves.

00:11:52.310 --> 00:11:55.690
And, it's a longterm, it's a marathon.

00:11:55.690 --> 00:11:57.010
It doesn't fix everything overnight.

00:11:57.010 --> 00:11:58.170
But if in the longterm,

00:11:58.170 --> 00:11:59.100
if Khan Academy is able

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:00.180
to reach billions of folks

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:01.550
and really empower them,

00:12:01.550 --> 00:12:03.420
I think we can,

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:04.430
make our positive dent

00:12:04.430 --> 00:12:05.880
in the universe, so to speak.

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:10.210
Let's see so Tina Lou says,

00:12:10.210 --> 00:12:12.620
how many books do you read every year?

00:12:12.620 --> 00:12:15.224
I'm not like a,

00:12:15.224 --> 00:12:18.370
there's famous people like Bill Gates

00:12:18.370 --> 00:12:19.310
who are known to,

00:12:19.310 --> 00:12:20.320
kind of speed read,

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:22.434
consume books that is racist.

00:12:22.434 --> 00:12:25.780
Personally, I'm not that fast of a reader.

00:12:25.780 --> 00:12:27.190
And, I'll say that

00:12:27.190 --> 00:12:29.214
just so other non fast
readers feel comfortable

00:12:29.214 --> 00:12:30.780
with things that they're not fast.

00:12:30.780 --> 00:12:32.490
I have very good comprehension,

00:12:32.490 --> 00:12:33.610
but maybe that's a byproduct

00:12:33.610 --> 00:12:34.670
because I actually don't read.

00:12:34.670 --> 00:12:35.960
I'm probably the slowest reader.

00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:37.240
My wife reads faster than me

00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:39.100
my mother-in-law reads faster than me.

00:12:39.100 --> 00:12:40.820
Probably many of y'all
read faster than me,

00:12:40.820 --> 00:12:43.230
but I definitely immerse myself

00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:44.063
when I'm in a book

00:12:44.063 --> 00:12:44.896
when I'm in a book,

00:12:44.896 --> 00:12:46.210
I definitely like,

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:47.043
I'm in it.

00:12:47.043 --> 00:12:48.780
Like, I'm in that reality.

00:12:48.780 --> 00:12:49.613
And, and that's probably

00:12:49.613 --> 00:12:51.600
why I read a little bit slower.

00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:52.870
But, it depends,

00:12:52.870 --> 00:12:53.990
I probably read

00:12:53.990 --> 00:12:56.000
a handful of books a month,

00:12:56.000 --> 00:12:58.760
maybe, right now I'm reading "Shantaram",

00:12:58.760 --> 00:13:00.150
which is a 900 page book,

00:13:00.150 --> 00:13:03.220
and I'm reading maybe 30 minutes a night.

00:13:03.220 --> 00:13:04.890
So that's taking me more than

00:13:04.890 --> 00:13:06.040
a couple of weeks to get through,

00:13:06.040 --> 00:13:07.883
but I'm really enjoying it.

00:13:09.810 --> 00:13:10.650
But sometimes there's quick.

00:13:10.650 --> 00:13:12.330
Yes, I would say probably

00:13:12.330 --> 00:13:15.003
about two or three books a month.

00:13:15.850 --> 00:13:16.683
It sounds about right.

00:13:16.683 --> 00:13:17.516
Sometimes it's last,

00:13:17.516 --> 00:13:19.360
maybe one, sometimes it's
at the high end of that,

00:13:19.360 --> 00:13:20.853
but that's about what I do.

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:25.120
So let's see,

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:30.120
we have a question from John Cheryl

00:13:30.360 --> 00:13:32.020
from Facebook, any suggestions

00:13:32.020 --> 00:13:35.730
to get a 13 year old
boy hooked on reading?

00:13:35.730 --> 00:13:36.637
I have ideas.

00:13:36.637 --> 00:13:38.640
I'm not, the expert here,

00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:40.210
but I could even try

00:13:40.210 --> 00:13:43.933
to channel my own 13
year old self because,

00:13:45.200 --> 00:13:46.170
it even goes to the last question.

00:13:46.170 --> 00:13:47.810
I actually was not a strong reader

00:13:47.810 --> 00:13:48.950
when I was in elementary school.

00:13:48.950 --> 00:13:50.700
When I was in second grade,

00:13:50.700 --> 00:13:51.533
up until second grade,

00:13:51.533 --> 00:13:52.880
I was actually in speech therapy.

00:13:52.880 --> 00:13:54.794
'Cause I couldn't even
communicate properly.

00:13:54.794 --> 00:13:57.650
My sister was in the gifted

00:13:57.650 --> 00:13:58.530
and talented program

00:13:58.530 --> 00:13:59.900
where they took her out of class.

00:13:59.900 --> 00:14:00.839
She was three years older than me.

00:14:00.839 --> 00:14:03.797
And when they started
taking me out of class,

00:14:03.797 --> 00:14:07.300
I assumed that surely I'm Ferris brother.

00:14:07.300 --> 00:14:08.730
I must be in this program as well.

00:14:08.730 --> 00:14:10.300
No, it turns out that
no one could understand

00:14:10.300 --> 00:14:11.133
what I was saying.

00:14:11.133 --> 00:14:12.916
So I remember going to these rooms

00:14:12.916 --> 00:14:15.470
where they would just play these tapes

00:14:15.470 --> 00:14:16.980
and make me repeat what they were saying.

00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:19.550
And, people still didn't understand,

00:14:19.550 --> 00:14:20.383
what I was saying.

00:14:20.383 --> 00:14:23.130
And so I had gaps in communication.

00:14:23.130 --> 00:14:25.060
I couldn't, I wasn't
that strong of a reader.

00:14:25.060 --> 00:14:28.310
I actually probably
didn't really read a full,

00:14:28.310 --> 00:14:29.300
proper book until I

00:14:29.300 --> 00:14:31.178
was near late elementary school

00:14:31.178 --> 00:14:32.870
or even in middle school.

00:14:32.870 --> 00:14:34.490
So I no one who knew me

00:14:34.490 --> 00:14:35.323
when I was eight

00:14:35.323 --> 00:14:37.090
or nine years old would've thought

00:14:37.090 --> 00:14:38.080
that I would have become

00:14:38.080 --> 00:14:39.500
a voracious reader one day

00:14:39.500 --> 00:14:41.600
or maybe are probably even surprised

00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:43.960
that I would have even
gotten into education.

00:14:43.960 --> 00:14:45.500
But in middle school,

00:14:45.500 --> 00:14:47.820
I think, there's a lot of great books

00:14:47.820 --> 00:14:50.180
that you're exposed to in middle school.

00:14:50.180 --> 00:14:51.260
But they do tend to,

00:14:51.260 --> 00:14:52.093
or I don't know,

00:14:52.093 --> 00:14:53.290
I'm sure things have changed a bit now,

00:14:53.290 --> 00:14:54.750
but when I was in middle school,

00:14:54.750 --> 00:14:56.990
they did tend to be a little bit more of

00:14:56.990 --> 00:14:58.960
the Victorian era classics,

00:14:58.960 --> 00:15:00.270
which now as an adult,

00:15:00.270 --> 00:15:01.190
I really appreciate it.

00:15:01.190 --> 00:15:02.500
I mean, I said one of my favorite books

00:15:02.500 --> 00:15:04.640
is Jane Austen's "Pride and Prejudice",

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:07.210
but I think for a middle school boys,

00:15:07.210 --> 00:15:09.580
especially some of the
great science fiction works,

00:15:09.580 --> 00:15:11.410
not to stereotype or generalize.

00:15:11.410 --> 00:15:12.850
'Cause I think a lot of girls,

00:15:12.850 --> 00:15:15.170
young girls like to read these as well.

00:15:15.170 --> 00:15:18.900
But, when I first read
the Foundation series

00:15:18.900 --> 00:15:20.520
and I read that in middle school

00:15:21.560 --> 00:15:22.930
when I first read some of

00:15:22.930 --> 00:15:25.070
the "Arthur C. Clark",

00:15:25.070 --> 00:15:26.623
I read "Dune" in high school,

00:15:27.670 --> 00:15:30.010
I always appreciated the Victorian era,

00:15:30.010 --> 00:15:31.430
like the way they observe

00:15:31.430 --> 00:15:33.070
the nuances of society

00:15:33.070 --> 00:15:34.110
and all that,

00:15:34.110 --> 00:15:36.130
but also to have this other dimension

00:15:36.130 --> 00:15:37.500
of like space opera

00:15:37.500 --> 00:15:39.890
and like how epic the universe could be

00:15:39.890 --> 00:15:41.227
and our roles in it.

00:15:41.227 --> 00:15:43.380
And just think about
reality in different ways.

00:15:43.380 --> 00:15:45.670
I think that just gives
you another dimension.

00:15:45.670 --> 00:15:47.922
So my advice long winded way of saying,

00:15:47.922 --> 00:15:50.650
expose your son to more types of books

00:15:50.650 --> 00:15:53.650
and see what he resonates with.

00:15:53.650 --> 00:15:55.840
And sometimes, there's this gravity

00:15:55.840 --> 00:15:59.640
that doing the very high
minded academic classic stuff.

00:15:59.640 --> 00:16:00.770
But I think there's just a beauty

00:16:00.770 --> 00:16:02.270
in just getting started reading.

00:16:02.270 --> 00:16:04.170
And once you start
immersing yourself in books

00:16:04.170 --> 00:16:05.190
and you realize that

00:16:05.190 --> 00:16:07.110
it's the best form of virtual reality

00:16:07.110 --> 00:16:09.610
and probably the cheapest
form of virtual reality,

00:16:10.740 --> 00:16:13.420
then I think you can get your son hooked

00:16:13.420 --> 00:16:14.850
and then he'll start exploring things

00:16:14.850 --> 00:16:16.750
that might be a little bit out

00:16:16.750 --> 00:16:19.080
of his normal comfort zone.

00:16:19.080 --> 00:16:21.673
So that's my best guess.

00:16:22.890 --> 00:16:27.890
Let's see, there's a Nikhil Guvendor.

00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:29.370
Do I enjoy computer science?

00:16:29.370 --> 00:16:30.203
And if so,

00:16:30.203 --> 00:16:31.960
what's my favorite programming language.

00:16:31.960 --> 00:16:33.030
Well, I do, I mean,

00:16:33.030 --> 00:16:34.590
I was a computer science major in college

00:16:34.590 --> 00:16:35.530
and the reason why I did that,

00:16:35.530 --> 00:16:36.870
it was, this was before

00:16:36.870 --> 00:16:37.970
it was obvious that computer science

00:16:37.970 --> 00:16:39.973
was a very pragmatic field to go into.

00:16:39.973 --> 00:16:43.220
It was because I originally went

00:16:43.220 --> 00:16:44.540
into college wanting to be a physics major

00:16:44.540 --> 00:16:46.720
because I wanted to understand reality

00:16:46.720 --> 00:16:48.280
and I still do.

00:16:48.280 --> 00:16:50.320
But what was captivating

00:16:50.320 --> 00:16:51.870
about computer science is that

00:16:51.870 --> 00:16:52.790
it's kind of a world

00:16:52.790 --> 00:16:54.480
where you can create a reality

00:16:54.480 --> 00:16:55.830
and you can create tools

00:16:55.830 --> 00:16:59.150
that can scale arbitrarily to millions

00:16:59.150 --> 00:17:00.770
and millions of folks.

00:17:00.770 --> 00:17:02.960
And so, that's what got me

00:17:02.960 --> 00:17:04.170
into computer science

00:17:04.170 --> 00:17:05.450
and obviously to some degree helped

00:17:05.450 --> 00:17:07.277
me get Khan Academy going

00:17:07.277 --> 00:17:11.003
and kind of those early
prototypes of our platform.

00:17:12.360 --> 00:17:13.410
I don't, say I have

00:17:13.410 --> 00:17:14.610
a favorite programming language.

00:17:14.610 --> 00:17:16.010
I was use a programming languages

00:17:16.010 --> 00:17:17.100
as a means to an end.

00:17:17.100 --> 00:17:20.180
I not that I have a deep love for Python

00:17:20.180 --> 00:17:21.823
over Java over,

00:17:22.800 --> 00:17:24.456
few plus plus or whatever else.

00:17:24.456 --> 00:17:26.840
For me it's a mean to an end,

00:17:26.840 --> 00:17:28.980
which language is a better tool

00:17:28.980 --> 00:17:31.630
for what I'm trying to
accomplish, at that.

00:17:31.630 --> 00:17:33.240
I've always programmed more

00:17:33.240 --> 00:17:35.270
for the sake of trying to create something

00:17:35.270 --> 00:17:36.130
that could be useful

00:17:36.130 --> 00:17:37.790
or interesting versus kind

00:17:37.790 --> 00:17:40.220
of just the programming
for programming sake.

00:17:40.220 --> 00:17:41.053
But I can definitely see

00:17:41.053 --> 00:17:42.450
how people are attracted to

00:17:42.450 --> 00:17:44.410
the beauty of the programming itself.

00:17:44.410 --> 00:17:46.200
'Cause there is something very beautiful

00:17:46.200 --> 00:17:48.883
about kind of a logical structures.

00:17:49.780 --> 00:17:51.080
And I think it does really help

00:17:51.080 --> 00:17:52.750
you learn to think critically

00:17:52.750 --> 00:17:55.410
and logically regardless

00:17:55.410 --> 00:17:57.783
of whether you end up
becoming a programmer or not.

00:17:58.820 --> 00:18:00.450
Let's see from YouTube,

